WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.980
- Expression my mother often uses,

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:04.490
that you do the right
things for the right reasons

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:06.290
and you live with the consequences.

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:08.480
(upbeat music)

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.059
- [Man] You got your perspective.

00:00:10.059 --> 00:00:11.410
(car revving)
(upbeat music continues)

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:13.485
(crowd cheering)

00:00:13.485 --> 00:00:14.318
I just wanna be happy.

00:00:14.318 --> 00:00:15.178
Don't you want to be happy?

00:00:15.178 --> 00:00:18.550
(lively cheerful music)

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:20.750
Everybody's Gary Vaynerchuk.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:24.220
Really exciting day for me
because I admire the gentlemen

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:26.000
that I will be interviewing quite a bit

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.340
and have for a very long time.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:30.810
And we spent two seconds
getting to know each other

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:31.940
talking of course football

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:34.170
and we'll get into that in a minute.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:36.810
But as many of you know,
who follow my content,

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:38.450
I haven't done a whole
lot of interviews lately.

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:41.980
And to be Frank, I think that
interacting with all of you

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:43.940
and answering your questions
has been really fruitful.

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:47.370
And I wanna save the
interviews on the podcast

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:49.950
for special occasions, one that excites me

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:50.783
or one that I think

00:00:50.783 --> 00:00:52.470
can disproportionately
bring value to the audience

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:54.500
and this showman hits on both fronts.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:55.970
So without further ado,

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:58.970
I'd like to introduce,
Arthur Blank to the show.

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:03.010
I will let this prestigious
gentlemen introduce himself

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:03.870
'cause I'm curious to hear

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:05.560
how he positions himself these days.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:06.970
And he's got a new book out

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:10.830
and I know how much my
audience has enjoyed

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:13.240
getting to know authors around books

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:14.600
and have been big supporters.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:18.200
So I hope the Vayner nation
can climb some Amazon

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.600
and Barnes and Noble's ratings
and things of that nature.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:22.970
So, excited for the
new book, good company.

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:25.100
So, Arthur, how are you?

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:26.440
- I'm fine.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:30.520
It's an honor to chat
with you for a little bit.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:31.870
The other thing which we share

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:33.680
and we probably share a
lot of things in common,

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:36.082
but we both came from Queens.

00:01:36.082 --> 00:01:36.915
- That's right.

00:01:36.915 --> 00:01:40.280
- And so I have that diversity

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:43.120
and that life experience in my background.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:48.100
But today, I co-founded
The Home Depot in 1979,

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:51.360
their first stores and left in 2001.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.700
And we were the second
largest retail in the world

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:54.533
at that time.

00:01:54.533 --> 00:01:57.980
And today the company has a book value

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:00.527
or capital value I should
say, of close to 300 million,

00:02:00.527 --> 00:02:01.360
$300 billion.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:05.400
And I transitioned into
a variety of other things

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.150
to the Atlanta Falcons
which you mentioned,

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:09.660
Atlanta United, our
Major League Soccer team.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.760
We operate the PGA Tour Superstores

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.010
which is the largest golf
retail in America today.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:17.910
We operate several
guest ranches in Montana

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:21.810
rated number one in the
western part of the country.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:24.180
And we have a very active

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:27.670
and philanthropic family foundation.

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:29.670
So we're involved in a lot of things.

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:31.110
And I think the beauty of the book

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.010
and the essence of the
book is that all the values

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:37.150
that we cultivated and developed
at Home Depot for 23 years

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:40.540
are now being shepherd along
beautifully by Craig Menear

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:41.763
and their whole team.

00:02:42.894 --> 00:02:44.500
Are the same values we put in place

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:47.770
in all of these different
industries, in different settings

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:48.900
different geographies.

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:52.960
With fans, customers, and
guests, all three of them

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:55.520
through our associates and
connected to the communities

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:56.630
that we live in.

00:02:56.630 --> 00:02:58.920
And really the emphasis of the stories

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:01.440
and the thoughts
throughout the book is that

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.570
you can't have both profitability

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:05.730
and emphasis on the people
side of the equation.

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:07.653
In fact, if you're gonna have,

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:11.070
really gonna achieve both at high levels

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:13.000
you need to achieve both.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:14.870
You need to put emphasis on both of them

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:16.730
and one feeds the other very strongly.

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:20.100
So, it's a great book,
not because I wrote it.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.620
It's not really about me,
but it's about the values

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:26.100
which we think are transportable
to virtually every business

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.730
for profit or non-profit
as the case may be.

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:31.360
- Arthur, I think this is where we share

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:33.680
a lot of perspective.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:36.580
To me I call my VaynerX, my universe.

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:38.343
I call it the honey empire.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:41.760
I have enormous ambitions,

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:45.280
I'd like to match the
accomplishments you have.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:47.854
Especially 'cause I wanna
buy the New York Jets.

00:03:47.854 --> 00:03:50.410
(Arthur laughs)

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:53.110
And, but I also think that you can do that

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:54.394
with honey over vinegar.

00:03:54.394 --> 00:03:56.860
I'm uncomfortable actually,

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:00.900
In deep negativity and bad human traits.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:05.230
And I enjoy the part
of my career right now

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:06.610
because I have a lot of kids watching me

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:08.820
on these social networks and (indistinct).

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:11.710
I started a wine brand,
it's called Empathy.

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:15.890
Like these things to me and
you were pioneering it before.

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:17.070
I think people are confused

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:19.050
that you don't have to kill people

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:21.770
or be mean to be a
successful business person.

00:04:21.770 --> 00:04:24.980
I think that's what I heard
in your opening statement.

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:27.940
- Yeah, well, I think not only,

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:28.980
you don't have to be that way.

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:31.670
I think it's counter being successful.

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:35.900
And I'm measuring success
really in two ways.

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:38.250
One is the financial
results, which obviously

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:41.040
is what most people
conventionally would think of.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:45.060
But also in terms of
having purpose in life

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:49.310
and you don't have to be
someone like Deepak Chopra

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:51.830
who's a good friend and
endorsed the book and read it.

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:55.320
But you have to understand that

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:59.310
young people today particularly
I think are striving

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:01.800
for more purpose than I think

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:03.970
they've ever done so in the past.

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:06.350
An example and you're
probably familiar with this

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:09.310
is Dr. Laurie Santos,
who also read the book

00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:10.620
and endorses it.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:13.680
Who has a professor of
psychology at the University.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:16.010
She started this course,
as you probably know,

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:18.750
on happiness, her happiness lab,

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:21.050
because 30-40 kids said to her,

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:22.520
there has to be more
in life than just this

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:23.850
there has to be more in life

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:26.960
than just making money, et
cetera, and being successful.

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:29.770
She now, as many of your
listeners may be aware.

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:32.190
She now, she teaches that class

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.230
to a quarter of the student body

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:36.900
of Yale University at a time, one quarter.

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:39.100
It's largest enrollment
class they've ever had

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:40.600
in the history of Yale
- My God!

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:43.480
- 205 years old, and it's now
available to everybody online.

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:45.760
So you pass that onto your listeners too.

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:47.900
It's available online and
it's done beautifully.

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:48.733
So--

00:05:48.733 --> 00:05:49.610
- Arthur let me ask a question.

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:50.630
I'm sorry to interrupt
- Yeah

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:51.650
- But I wanna get so much out of you

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:53.050
in this short time together.

00:05:53.990 --> 00:05:55.786
What kind of kid were you?

00:05:55.786 --> 00:05:57.770
(Arthur laughs)
Like I'm very, very curious.

00:05:57.770 --> 00:05:59.350
- Well I was very competitive.

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:00.917
I was very competitive.

00:06:00.917 --> 00:06:03.610
And my focus really growing up was sports.

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:06.420
I played football, ran
track, played baseball,

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:07.253
for my high school.

00:06:07.253 --> 00:06:10.620
And I was really a good
athlete I would say.

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:12.780
Not good enough to go to the next level.

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:15.270
And I pretty much banged
myself up in my junior year

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:16.103
playing football.

00:06:16.103 --> 00:06:17.210
We had a great football team

00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:19.800
at Stuyvesant High School in New York.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:22.500
My junior year we won all of our games.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:24.430
Average score was like 40-46,

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:26.000
I think something like that.
(Gary laughs)

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:27.640
So we had a really good team.

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:29.870
We had a bunch of young men that went on

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:32.200
and got full scholarships and played well

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:35.920
and collegiately, but I've
always been competitive--

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:36.760
- Were your...

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:38.290
So I don't know if you know this

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:39.270
'cause I know you knew,

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:40.790
it sounds like you knew the Queen's part.

00:06:40.790 --> 00:06:42.470
But I was actually born in Belarus

00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:44.340
in the former Soviet Union.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:45.173
- No, I didn't know that.

00:06:45.173 --> 00:06:47.784
- Yeah, and I came over in 78

00:06:47.784 --> 00:06:50.500
when they let a small amount of Jews out,

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:51.510
when Israel and America,

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:52.960
you might remember this Jury
- I do.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:54.280
- Was able to get...

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:56.650
So that is my origin story

00:06:56.650 --> 00:06:59.690
and I've always felt, and I
tell a lot of my contemporaries

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:01.340
in their late 30s, early 40s

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:03.580
that are business men
and women (indistinct)

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:04.600
When we get into these dinners,

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:05.620
I'm like you have to understand,

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:07.660
I'm far more like your grandfather

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:10.650
than I am like you, because
I lived in a studio apartment

00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:12.810
with seven family members.

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:13.990
Like I remember that.

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:15.380
This is not my...

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:20.380
I think that you grew up
in a Queens, in your years.

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:23.970
Hey, was it your parents or grandparents

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:25.910
that came over from the old country?

00:07:25.910 --> 00:07:27.060
- It was my grandparents.

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:30.380
- Right, so to me, I'm curious.

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:33.860
How much do you think the environment

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:37.500
that you grew up in lent
itself to the acceleration

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:38.340
of your competitiveness?

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:40.477
Because it's obviously a do or die game,

00:07:40.477 --> 00:07:43.090
but it's also clearly an environment game.

00:07:43.090 --> 00:07:44.560
And looking at your timing

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:46.280
of when you were growing up in Queens,

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:49.920
I'm always romantic about
that era as a historian,

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:52.170
'cause I think a lot of
good chips on shoulders

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:55.950
and ruggedness that led to
great success to your point

00:07:55.950 --> 00:08:00.390
both happy and algorithmic
and kind of financial.

00:08:00.390 --> 00:08:02.580
There's a lot of men and
women that came from that era.

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:04.890
And there's something in the water,

00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:06.340
something you hear a lot.

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:08.290
I feel like there's something in the water

00:08:08.290 --> 00:08:11.950
for those Italian, Jews,
Irishmen, immigrants, kids,

00:08:11.950 --> 00:08:15.320
and grandkids of immigrants
in that New York at your era.

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:17.350
- Well, that's a really good point.

00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:21.490
And I think, yes there was
something in the water.

00:08:21.490 --> 00:08:24.680
And I think the water at
that point was going through

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:27.040
the experience of your
parents coming through

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:29.580
Ellis Island, settling in lower Manhattan,

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:32.920
often without anything
to speak of financially.

00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:35.460
We were working any number of jobs

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:40.460
to just maintain a sense
of keeping family together

00:08:40.670 --> 00:08:41.770
and supporting each other.

00:08:41.770 --> 00:08:45.940
And so, I think the notion of overcoming

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:47.700
that form of adversity, if you will,

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:48.580
or those challenges,

00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:50.700
I wouldn't say adversity, but challenges.

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:53.680
I think we've really done
beautifully by our families.

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:57.200
And so I think in my family,

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:00.470
given the fact I lost my dad
when I was only 15 years old

00:09:00.470 --> 00:09:01.553
he was 44.

00:09:01.553 --> 00:09:05.380
And so I think that that puts
some emphasis on my mind--

00:09:05.380 --> 00:09:08.370
- By the way, I have another
connection point for us.

00:09:08.370 --> 00:09:11.320
And I realized my father, at the same ages

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:13.640
on both his father and himself,

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:15.280
lost his dad at 15, when my grandfather

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:17.022
was 44, yeah.
- Oh wow!

00:09:17.022 --> 00:09:17.855
That's amazing, wow!

00:09:17.855 --> 00:09:19.903
Well, that's a real connection here.

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:24.730
So, my mother went into his
business without any background

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:27.060
that he had started in his early forties

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:29.730
and she ran it beautifully
and eventually sold it.

00:09:29.730 --> 00:09:33.500
And so I think the notion of
pushing through challenges,

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:36.628
looking for challenges,
looking for opportunities

00:09:36.628 --> 00:09:40.053
to enhance, to make the world
a better place, if you will.

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:42.670
My mother was a woman of principle.

00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:44.810
I think that became very important to me

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:48.230
at an early age and the
expression my mother often uses

00:09:48.230 --> 00:09:50.750
that you do the right
things for the right reasons

00:09:50.750 --> 00:09:53.000
and you live with the consequences.

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:56.438
I think that stayed with me for many years

00:09:56.438 --> 00:09:59.500
and drives a lot of the
the cultural aspects

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:01.310
of what we talk about in the book.

00:10:01.310 --> 00:10:02.980
- I'm gonna stop you there because I think

00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:05.600
when people ask me why I
have this honey empire thing?

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:06.800
Blah, blah, blah, blah.

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:09.020
And I literally say my mom,

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:10.560
my mom...

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:11.860
First of all, I share her DNA.

00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:14.320
So being nice and kind.

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:16.440
And by the way, I'm
uncomfortably competitive

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:18.160
which actually confuses people.

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:19.570
Actually, it's a good question, Arthur,

00:10:19.570 --> 00:10:24.060
since you're many years
and success ahead of me

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:26.440
on this journey, but I'm very curious.

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:28.120
And maybe it's the
climate we live in today,

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:31.340
'cause obviously, it's
always a timing thing.

00:10:31.340 --> 00:10:34.690
But, do you feel that people actually

00:10:34.690 --> 00:10:37.610
confuse individuals that are competitive

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:40.410
and don't realize that
it's actually very easy

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:43.160
to coexist with kindness
and competitiveness,

00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:45.510
but that sometimes competitiveness

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:48.280
especially in the heat
of the moment manifests

00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:50.210
to something that seems a
little bit more aggressive

00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:51.710
and not naturally kind,

00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:53.920
And that confuses some outside observers?

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:55.070
- Yeah, I think it can.

00:10:55.070 --> 00:10:59.710
But, people that I have great respect for

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:05.287
even making, being as
firm as they want to be

00:11:05.287 --> 00:11:07.450
in making the points they need to make

00:11:07.450 --> 00:11:09.850
and strong as need to make it,

00:11:09.850 --> 00:11:11.520
have always found a way to do it

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:13.900
with a degree of kindness.

00:11:13.900 --> 00:11:15.450
And I go back to Dr. King

00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:19.320
two of his closest disciples
are close friends of mind.

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:21.520
One just passed away, John Lewis,

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:23.630
who was a very close friend

00:11:23.630 --> 00:11:25.560
and Andy Yango still lives in Atlanta.

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:27.863
Two of the closest to Dr. King.

00:11:27.863 --> 00:11:29.750
And another gentleman brought to fore

00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:31.320
in the book, Jimmy Carter.

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:34.610
One of the kindest thoughtful,
most caring people I've met

00:11:34.610 --> 00:11:37.310
in my life and yet extremely competitive.

00:11:37.310 --> 00:11:39.620
I mean, there's a story
I talk about in the book

00:11:39.620 --> 00:11:43.520
where we were climbing rocks
together within a bound course

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:47.470
and his wife got up on
top sooner than he did.

00:11:47.470 --> 00:11:51.080
So I said, Mr President, this
was after he left office,

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:52.720
have you had enough?

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:53.890
I remember he looked at me,

00:11:53.890 --> 00:11:56.130
sweaty, sweaty, sweaty eyes, everything.

00:11:56.130 --> 00:11:56.963
He said, no.

00:11:56.963 --> 00:11:59.560
He said, we're not leaving
until I make this climb.

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:04.080
And he did, but, so I
think you can have both.

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:04.913
- It's funny, I agree.

00:12:04.913 --> 00:12:07.000
But the way I think about it and obviously

00:12:08.030 --> 00:12:09.790
a lot of people don't realize
how much success you have

00:12:09.790 --> 00:12:12.650
in soccer, proper football
with your club there too.

00:12:12.650 --> 00:12:15.160
I do think the physical manifestation

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:17.820
versus the boardroom communication style

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:19.660
does have a little bit of a tweak, right?

00:12:19.660 --> 00:12:21.340
I see a lot of people to your point

00:12:21.340 --> 00:12:23.930
that I see do a great job in the boardroom

00:12:23.930 --> 00:12:26.860
managing their people, managing
up, down, left and right.

00:12:26.860 --> 00:12:28.390
But then you get them
in to pickup basketball

00:12:28.390 --> 00:12:29.770
and they're throwing
an elbow in your chest

00:12:29.770 --> 00:12:31.390
'cause that's where you get to see their

00:12:31.390 --> 00:12:32.777
physical manifestation.

00:12:32.777 --> 00:12:33.610
- Well, yeah.

00:12:33.610 --> 00:12:34.443
(crosstalk)

00:12:34.443 --> 00:12:36.700
I think that I can relate to that.

00:12:36.700 --> 00:12:38.650
And I would say (indistinct)
(Gary laughs)

00:12:38.650 --> 00:12:41.480
I was always very competitive
and I still am today.

00:12:41.480 --> 00:12:43.623
I don't like to lose anything, I remember,

00:12:44.770 --> 00:12:47.630
A brief story, I remember my wife and I,

00:12:47.630 --> 00:12:49.990
first year we got married,
we played Scrabble.

00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:51.520
We played 24, 24.

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:55.230
The first one who won 25 games

00:12:55.230 --> 00:12:56.707
is gonna be the champion of Scrabble.

00:12:56.707 --> 00:12:58.810
And I told this to a team,

00:12:58.810 --> 00:13:02.400
a football team in 2001, they
asked me, are you competitive?

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:04.110
So we played the last game of Scrabble

00:13:04.110 --> 00:13:05.960
with no time limits on it.

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:07.180
I lost that.

00:13:07.180 --> 00:13:08.810
We were married 27 years.

00:13:08.810 --> 00:13:10.680
We never played another game of Scrabble.

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:11.797
- I love you already.

00:13:11.797 --> 00:13:15.270
- So, I don't like to lose
(Gary laughs)

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:18.097
but, anyway, but it's--

00:13:19.910 --> 00:13:21.660
- Remind me, is the book out

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:23.216
or about to be out.
- It's out.

00:13:23.216 --> 00:13:24.220
- It's out--

00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:26.620
- It's out, it was
published September 15th

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:29.200
and it's out available on
all the booking outlets

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:30.391
you can imagine.

00:13:30.391 --> 00:13:34.170
- What has been the most surprising?

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:37.170
For me, I've written five
business books and one wine book

00:13:37.170 --> 00:13:38.863
I grew up in the wine
business of all things.

00:13:38.863 --> 00:13:41.670
My dad has a liquor store in
New Jersey that I transformed

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:43.370
to an early dot-com wine business.

00:13:44.860 --> 00:13:47.270
The one thing I liked with
all six of those processes

00:13:47.270 --> 00:13:48.900
was the feedback.

00:13:48.900 --> 00:13:52.100
'Cause you're in your
book for a period of time.

00:13:52.100 --> 00:13:54.520
The feedback that you didn't see coming.

00:13:54.520 --> 00:13:55.840
I think this may be a fun question

00:13:55.840 --> 00:13:57.490
that might have some insights.

00:13:57.490 --> 00:14:01.820
Any feedback from a random email

00:14:01.820 --> 00:14:05.310
or somebody close to you of
an observation from the book

00:14:05.310 --> 00:14:07.640
that you had not thought
of but you appreciated

00:14:07.640 --> 00:14:10.210
or that kind of has caught
your attention so far here

00:14:10.210 --> 00:14:11.605
in the couple of weeks that it's been out.

00:14:11.605 --> 00:14:13.383
- Well, I think it's a great question.

00:14:13.383 --> 00:14:15.680
I don't know, but I'll
be seeking the answer.

00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:16.513
- Okay.

00:14:16.513 --> 00:14:18.860
- Because I have told
people who've read it

00:14:18.860 --> 00:14:21.320
or halfway through it or whatever,

00:14:21.320 --> 00:14:24.900
I'm really interested in
their critique of the book.

00:14:24.900 --> 00:14:27.500
I mean as you've experienced,

00:14:27.500 --> 00:14:28.800
when you write one of these books

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:31.510
and you go through them,
after a while you fall in love

00:14:31.510 --> 00:14:32.350
with what you're writing

00:14:32.350 --> 00:14:34.413
and you finally sign off on it.

00:14:34.413 --> 00:14:35.440
It's the best product

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:37.320
that you can produce at
that time, et cetera.

00:14:37.320 --> 00:14:39.420
And it tells your story,
whatever story it may be.

00:14:39.420 --> 00:14:42.883
But how other people see it,

00:14:42.883 --> 00:14:43.750
and there are a handful of people

00:14:43.750 --> 00:14:46.100
that I have deep respect for

00:14:46.100 --> 00:14:48.160
that I'm interested in
getting their feedback.

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:50.310
And I'm sure they'll
give it to me too, so.

00:14:51.930 --> 00:14:55.780
- Do you look at this as a legacy piece?

00:14:55.780 --> 00:14:58.560
Because you feel like that
one of your natural talents.

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:00.280
And, by the way, I'm such a buyer

00:15:00.280 --> 00:15:01.940
of an important thing you said.

00:15:01.940 --> 00:15:05.030
I have literally told every CFO,

00:15:05.030 --> 00:15:07.970
overly financially driven friend
of mine, because I tend to,

00:15:07.970 --> 00:15:09.700
even though I have these big ambitions,

00:15:09.700 --> 00:15:10.980
obsessed with the process,

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:14.300
more than actually the dollars
that come along with it.

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:15.730
I've told them all the time,

00:15:15.730 --> 00:15:17.640
I believe that kindness actually

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:19.990
is a practical financial impact there.

00:15:19.990 --> 00:15:22.710
And, my heart was smiling
and my brain was smiling,

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:24.360
when you said the same.

00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:26.380
Do you view this as a
legacy piece for you?

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:29.350
Because you do think that it is a,

00:15:29.350 --> 00:15:31.390
it's a nuance that comes natural to you,

00:15:31.390 --> 00:15:34.110
that you've executed on
and your partners at Depot.

00:15:34.110 --> 00:15:35.710
Do you feel like this is something

00:15:35.710 --> 00:15:39.072
that you think is
misunderstood (indistinct)

00:15:39.072 --> 00:15:42.040
- Yeah I do Gary, but
it's interesting because

00:15:43.785 --> 00:15:46.490
it was just last year 2019,

00:15:46.490 --> 00:15:48.410
probably a lot of the people
that you're referring to

00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:50.010
or maybe some of the
people you're referring to

00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:51.460
were part of the business round table.

00:15:51.460 --> 00:15:55.370
So in 2019, after 50 years
of being in business,

00:15:55.370 --> 00:15:58.410
that round table, they
changed their charter

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:01.270
to saying, our emphasis
is not just gonna be

00:16:01.270 --> 00:16:04.210
return on profitability.

00:16:04.210 --> 00:16:06.300
It's gonna be people, planet and profits.

00:16:06.300 --> 00:16:09.500
All balanced out, keeping
in mind our associates

00:16:09.500 --> 00:16:11.560
and our suppliers and
our communities as well.

00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:15.970
So even that group of 192 CEOs,

00:16:15.970 --> 00:16:17.500
even they have changed their emphasis.

00:16:17.500 --> 00:16:19.070
And it goes back into Milton Friedman.

00:16:19.070 --> 00:16:22.150
The great economist
wrote a book 50 years ago

00:16:22.150 --> 00:16:24.180
and said the purpose of a
corporation is to make money

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:25.620
for shareholders, period.

00:16:25.620 --> 00:16:27.350
That's the end of the discussion.

00:16:27.350 --> 00:16:30.120
Today I think the view is different.

00:16:30.120 --> 00:16:31.730
It's changing, I think.

00:16:31.730 --> 00:16:33.860
And that's really the
emphasis of the book,

00:16:33.860 --> 00:16:35.900
is that I think you can have both.

00:16:35.900 --> 00:16:38.710
And I think you need both,
one enhances the other.

00:16:38.710 --> 00:16:40.160
It's not that you have to have one

00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:42.720
or the other or one takes
away from the other.

00:16:42.720 --> 00:16:44.950
The beauty of a weighted
barbell on both ends

00:16:44.950 --> 00:16:47.680
that is balanced is that,
it's equally weighted.

00:16:47.680 --> 00:16:49.800
And I think the story in this book is that

00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:53.060
those good values, that
based on human relationships,

00:16:53.060 --> 00:16:55.740
between who you're serving,
who's doing the serving

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:58.660
and the community you're
living in and profitability

00:16:58.660 --> 00:17:00.310
are all equally important.

00:17:00.310 --> 00:17:02.650
At the end of the day, you want people

00:17:02.650 --> 00:17:04.910
that work for you, Gary,
or people that work for me

00:17:04.910 --> 00:17:07.840
to feel like this is not
just about making money.

00:17:07.840 --> 00:17:11.970
It's about I'm committing
myself to an organization,

00:17:11.970 --> 00:17:13.770
that's making a difference in the world.

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:17.030
That's making a difference
in other people's lives.

00:17:17.030 --> 00:17:18.900
And you're following comments

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:21.000
because those people are listening to you.

00:17:22.170 --> 00:17:24.080
'cause they believe what
you're sharing with them

00:17:24.080 --> 00:17:26.081
is making a difference in their lives

00:17:26.081 --> 00:17:26.914
and you are happy to share with them--

00:17:26.914 --> 00:17:29.030
- To your point, I'm reverse engineering,

00:17:29.030 --> 00:17:32.500
bringing value for them, not myself,

00:17:32.500 --> 00:17:34.440
which I understand the karma of that.

00:17:34.440 --> 00:17:36.110
It has a practical impact

00:17:36.110 --> 00:17:38.350
on giving the opportunity for myself.

00:17:38.350 --> 00:17:39.210
It's not super complicated.

00:17:39.210 --> 00:17:41.380
It's good builds on good.

00:17:41.380 --> 00:17:42.213
- Exactly right.

00:17:42.213 --> 00:17:45.610
And that's, when you develop
that kind of relationship

00:17:45.610 --> 00:17:46.480
with who you're serving.

00:17:46.480 --> 00:17:49.100
So, like in our stadium, as an example,

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:50.700
we haven't had a couple of good years,

00:17:50.700 --> 00:17:52.460
our soccer team has been super relative

00:17:52.460 --> 00:17:56.470
since we started the franchise
three and a half years ago.

00:17:56.470 --> 00:17:57.960
The last couple of years our football team

00:17:57.960 --> 00:18:01.000
has been very average compared
to the Jets, maybe better,

00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:02.115
but not a whole lot better,
(Gary laughs)

00:18:02.115 --> 00:18:04.079
but not a whole lot better.

00:18:04.079 --> 00:18:06.035
- By the way.
- It's not been great.

00:18:06.035 --> 00:18:07.250
- By the way, I'm sorry to interrupt you.

00:18:07.250 --> 00:18:09.370
I know we were trying to do
this a couple of weeks ago.

00:18:09.370 --> 00:18:12.940
Schedules, when I, I don't
wanna even bring this up

00:18:12.940 --> 00:18:14.883
because I know how spicy I get after.

00:18:15.750 --> 00:18:18.370
I was watching your
football game this Sunday.

00:18:18.370 --> 00:18:20.750
And I said, Oh boy, this is,

00:18:20.750 --> 00:18:23.110
this dude is going to be so ...

00:18:23.110 --> 00:18:25.550
Because that one hurt.

00:18:25.550 --> 00:18:26.383
- Oh, it really hurt.

00:18:26.383 --> 00:18:28.830
I mean, it's just, when
you have that kind of lead,

00:18:28.830 --> 00:18:30.770
you have to go back.

00:18:30.770 --> 00:18:32.420
Your listeners probably
don't really wanna know this.

00:18:32.420 --> 00:18:33.890
The Jets may hold a similar record,

00:18:33.890 --> 00:18:36.170
but you have to go back to 1933

00:18:36.170 --> 00:18:39.500
when a team scored 39
points, had zero turnovers

00:18:39.500 --> 00:18:40.350
and lost the game.

00:18:40.350 --> 00:18:45.110
So, and on top of that, we had
turnover ratio of plus three.

00:18:45.110 --> 00:18:48.950
So, I mean, anyway,
we've already moved on.

00:18:48.950 --> 00:18:50.391
we gotta play the bears next Sunday.

00:18:50.391 --> 00:18:51.224
- That's right.

00:18:51.224 --> 00:18:53.077
- Coaches, players, we've all moved on--

00:18:53.077 --> 00:18:54.210
(crosstalk)

00:18:54.210 --> 00:18:55.510
- I wanna jump in here on this.

00:18:55.510 --> 00:18:56.573
Very curious.

00:18:58.070 --> 00:19:01.430
Has there been a challenge
transitioning the sports

00:19:01.430 --> 00:19:04.850
coming from the business
world, where it feels to me,

00:19:04.850 --> 00:19:06.680
and this is almost the
most selfish question

00:19:06.680 --> 00:19:09.410
in podcast history, because I'm
starting to mentally prepare

00:19:09.410 --> 00:19:11.883
for hopefully my great dream coming true.

00:19:14.110 --> 00:19:16.040
I'm already thinking through things like

00:19:16.040 --> 00:19:19.080
in my business life, I feel
like I have more control

00:19:19.080 --> 00:19:21.660
than I do with injuries and football.

00:19:21.660 --> 00:19:24.720
You gotta give a GM or a
coach, some breathing room

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:27.023
even when intuitively you're concerned.

00:19:28.070 --> 00:19:31.230
Just out of sheer curiosity,

00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:33.150
how does one find the balance

00:19:33.150 --> 00:19:35.490
of being able to control things?

00:19:35.490 --> 00:19:38.050
For example, somebody that has
a great interview with you.

00:19:38.050 --> 00:19:39.430
I don't know how many coaches you've had

00:19:39.430 --> 00:19:42.060
in your ownership, but
you've been through this.

00:19:42.060 --> 00:19:42.999
I've been through this.

00:19:42.999 --> 00:19:45.420
And even in my young
career, it feels right,

00:19:45.420 --> 00:19:46.370
right up to the second.

00:19:46.370 --> 00:19:49.240
And then literally a
week later you like, Oh!

00:19:49.240 --> 00:19:51.370
I'm trying to see that with
players, kids you've drafted.

00:19:51.370 --> 00:19:53.360
I've seen it, a million times over.

00:19:53.360 --> 00:19:54.840
My brother left my company

00:19:54.840 --> 00:19:56.910
and started an NFL
representation business.

00:19:56.910 --> 00:20:01.640
As a matter of fact, we have (indistinct)

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:04.160
which is my company represents your player

00:20:04.160 --> 00:20:08.659
that forced three fumbles
and one being this last week

00:20:08.659 --> 00:20:11.732
(crosstalk)

00:20:11.732 --> 00:20:14.060
- You're a very smart guy
and a terrific person.

00:20:14.060 --> 00:20:15.840
And obviously a great player.

00:20:15.840 --> 00:20:18.560
Probably, the coach once
said it probably be the best.

00:20:18.560 --> 00:20:22.100
Maybe the best 17 minutes
a linebacker has ever had--

00:20:22.100 --> 00:20:23.340
- By the way, even more fun.

00:20:23.340 --> 00:20:27.740
We also, we also represent
Keith D Beef, the fullback.

00:20:27.740 --> 00:20:30.050
And so--

00:20:30.050 --> 00:20:31.140
- A very nice young man

00:20:31.140 --> 00:20:35.713
- Very nice, so, I watch a lot
of your team because of that.

00:20:37.340 --> 00:20:40.040
Tell me what it took to calibrate

00:20:40.040 --> 00:20:43.900
the inability to act
when you wanted to act.

00:20:43.900 --> 00:20:45.300
Which I think is slightly different

00:20:45.300 --> 00:20:46.730
than running a company where

00:20:46.730 --> 00:20:49.000
then you have sometimes wait as well

00:20:49.000 --> 00:20:51.270
but not to the same
level of degrees I think

00:20:51.270 --> 00:20:53.350
when there's a sports franchise

00:20:53.350 --> 00:20:55.370
because of so many other variables in it.

00:20:55.370 --> 00:20:57.000
- Well, it's really interesting.

00:20:57.000 --> 00:21:00.230
I think that one good
piece of advice I got

00:21:00.230 --> 00:21:01.683
from Robert Kraft, who owns the Patriots,

00:21:01.683 --> 00:21:03.470
he's a good friend of mine.

00:21:03.470 --> 00:21:06.600
And when I bought the team in 2001,

00:21:06.600 --> 00:21:07.760
we had breakfast together.

00:21:07.760 --> 00:21:10.300
I was in New York with
commissioner (indistinct)

00:21:10.300 --> 00:21:11.950
going through my owns orientation.

00:21:11.950 --> 00:21:14.620
And he said, why don't
you hang around one day

00:21:14.620 --> 00:21:16.120
have breakfast tomorrow with Robert.

00:21:16.120 --> 00:21:17.580
And I said, I'd be happy to do that.

00:21:17.580 --> 00:21:18.900
So I did.

00:21:18.900 --> 00:21:20.290
And one of the things Robert said to me

00:21:20.290 --> 00:21:21.750
was that you're gonna
hear from a lot of people

00:21:21.750 --> 00:21:23.220
that the NFL is a different business.

00:21:23.220 --> 00:21:24.381
You're gonna hear that it's...

00:21:24.381 --> 00:21:26.700
Run the NFL, run your franchise,

00:21:26.700 --> 00:21:28.010
just like you're run the Home Depot

00:21:28.010 --> 00:21:28.960
and you're gonna be successful.

00:21:28.960 --> 00:21:30.620
Now the one thing that's different,

00:21:30.620 --> 00:21:31.590
two things are different.

00:21:31.590 --> 00:21:34.440
One of which you pointed out,
when somebody has an injury,

00:21:34.440 --> 00:21:36.390
God forbid if Matt Ryan gets hurt

00:21:36.390 --> 00:21:38.490
(crosstalk)

00:21:38.490 --> 00:21:39.700
The team is in a different place.

00:21:39.700 --> 00:21:42.260
Whereas in business,
Matt Ryan breaks a leg,

00:21:42.260 --> 00:21:44.260
he gets a cast on, he
comes to work the next day.

00:21:44.260 --> 00:21:45.378
So you're not losing him.

00:21:45.378 --> 00:21:46.211
- That's right.

00:21:46.211 --> 00:21:48.630
- The other thing which
is different is that

00:21:48.630 --> 00:21:51.780
the amount of media
coverage, which is involved

00:21:51.780 --> 00:21:54.730
with the NFL and therefore
all 32 teams is incredible.

00:21:54.730 --> 00:21:57.890
So when Robert said
that to me, I said well,

00:21:57.890 --> 00:21:59.870
Home Depot, we took the company public

00:21:59.870 --> 00:22:02.430
we're the second largest
retail in the world today.

00:22:02.430 --> 00:22:03.802
and, et cetera.

00:22:03.802 --> 00:22:06.280
(Crosstalk)

00:22:06.280 --> 00:22:07.920
He looked at me and he winked,

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:09.083
like you do, sometimes you do.

