00:24:57.823 --> 00:24:59.670
But, I follow people like Jay Shetty,

00:24:59.670 --> 00:25:02.070
I follow Deepak Chopra.

00:25:02.070 --> 00:25:03.820
They're both very good friends.

00:25:03.820 --> 00:25:07.050
There's some other people,
Simon Sinek, I follow.

00:25:07.050 --> 00:25:09.990
I mean, there are a lot of
people that I do follow.

00:25:09.990 --> 00:25:11.640
I'll follow you in the future.

00:25:11.640 --> 00:25:13.100
I will definitely follow
you in the future.

00:25:13.100 --> 00:25:14.060
I will.

00:25:14.060 --> 00:25:14.893
- Thank you.

00:25:14.893 --> 00:25:15.840
- Because you're covering
the kind of things

00:25:15.840 --> 00:25:17.030
and have the kind of personality

00:25:17.030 --> 00:25:19.000
that I would like to learn from.

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:21.890
So I think, (indistinct)
opportunities to learn from it.

00:25:21.890 --> 00:25:25.440
I mean, to me, there's a
story I tell them the book

00:25:25.440 --> 00:25:28.370
which, you may have picked
up you may not have.

00:25:28.370 --> 00:25:31.950
When we had an opportunity to
participate in these sports.

00:25:31.950 --> 00:25:35.530
And, it's a situation.

00:25:35.530 --> 00:25:36.700
I mean, we're a private company,

00:25:36.700 --> 00:25:38.420
we're not a public company, et cetera.

00:25:38.420 --> 00:25:41.700
And they came in and our
management team had already decided

00:25:41.700 --> 00:25:42.840
they really wanted to do this.

00:25:42.840 --> 00:25:44.090
They wanted to participate in it.

00:25:44.090 --> 00:25:45.810
They came up to the final presentation.

00:25:45.810 --> 00:25:48.560
The marketing folks came
in and they presented it.

00:25:48.560 --> 00:25:52.440
And one of the slides was
a picture of a young man

00:25:52.440 --> 00:25:55.740
who had made, as I remember
some ungodly amount of money,

00:25:55.740 --> 00:25:57.480
it was like 500,000 bucks.

00:25:57.480 --> 00:25:59.830
He was like 12 or 14 years old,

00:25:59.830 --> 00:26:02.337
but they said he practices 10 hours a day,

00:26:02.337 --> 00:26:05.480
10, 12 hours a day to be
able to be competitive

00:26:05.480 --> 00:26:06.980
at the level he's at.

00:26:06.980 --> 00:26:10.740
So when they left the
room, the presenting team,

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:12.790
our management team was over there.

00:26:12.790 --> 00:26:14.510
I said, we're not gonna do this.

00:26:14.510 --> 00:26:16.670
And they said, well, why
are we not gonna do it?

00:26:16.670 --> 00:26:18.310
I said, this has nothing
to do with our business,

00:26:18.310 --> 00:26:19.630
it has to do with our values.

00:26:19.630 --> 00:26:20.580
I said, I mean,

00:26:20.580 --> 00:26:24.720
I don't wanna encourage
young people honestly

00:26:24.720 --> 00:26:27.250
to sit in front of
technology, 10, 12 hours a day

00:26:27.250 --> 00:26:28.860
when I think they should be outside

00:26:28.860 --> 00:26:30.520
and they should be enjoying nature,

00:26:30.520 --> 00:26:32.670
enjoy playing with each
other with a lot of

00:26:32.670 --> 00:26:35.670
structured time or unstructured
time, whatever it may be.

00:26:35.670 --> 00:26:39.120
But I don't think it's to
their best interests long term.

00:26:39.120 --> 00:26:42.950
I do think, and you may or
may not agree with this.

00:26:42.950 --> 00:26:46.540
But I think Gary, that
the level of connectivity

00:26:46.540 --> 00:26:48.580
that we have today in the United States

00:26:48.580 --> 00:26:50.800
or throughout the world
is at an all time high

00:26:50.800 --> 00:26:52.820
and it's only gonna get more so.

00:26:52.820 --> 00:26:55.510
But the level of neighborhood
that exists today

00:26:55.510 --> 00:26:58.410
in the world is at an all time low.

00:26:58.410 --> 00:27:03.410
And I think back to the way you
and I were raised in Queens,

00:27:03.510 --> 00:27:05.010
the environment we were raised in,

00:27:05.010 --> 00:27:06.913
where we were nurtured in a community.

00:27:07.910 --> 00:27:10.520
Both in an apartment, I
had four family members

00:27:10.520 --> 00:27:12.070
living in one tiny apartment.

00:27:12.070 --> 00:27:14.110
You had eight, you lived in a community

00:27:14.110 --> 00:27:15.430
where everybody supported each other.

00:27:15.430 --> 00:27:17.660
Everybody came together, et cetera.

00:27:17.660 --> 00:27:19.560
We need more of that in this country--

00:27:20.787 --> 00:27:22.740
- To me, I think there's first of all

00:27:22.740 --> 00:27:24.610
from a value filter standpoint

00:27:24.610 --> 00:27:26.210
I couldn't agree with you more.

00:27:26.210 --> 00:27:27.850
I think my take to your point

00:27:27.850 --> 00:27:29.380
where it's skews a little bit different.

00:27:29.380 --> 00:27:32.470
First of all, the admiration
I have for people,

00:27:32.470 --> 00:27:35.510
I mean the amount of money
I have left on the table

00:27:35.510 --> 00:27:38.090
because I need to feel good about it

00:27:38.090 --> 00:27:40.600
is extraordinary and it is the single,

00:27:40.600 --> 00:27:42.130
and I'm gonna say this very clearly

00:27:42.130 --> 00:27:45.450
it is the single thing
that I'm most grateful for.

00:27:45.450 --> 00:27:47.330
It's just a nice feeling.

00:27:47.330 --> 00:27:50.160
There's no no payday
Interesting enough for me

00:27:50.160 --> 00:27:51.060
to feel that feeling.

00:27:51.060 --> 00:27:52.250
As a matter of fact, it's gonna lead

00:27:52.250 --> 00:27:54.610
to a different question I
wanted to ask you desperately,

00:27:54.610 --> 00:27:56.680
I just wanna get in my mind.

00:27:56.680 --> 00:27:59.890
What I would say, Arthur, is
what I've been able to discover

00:27:59.890 --> 00:28:02.150
because I've been so deep in the trenches.

00:28:02.150 --> 00:28:04.697
And I think like anything,
there's a great Russian saying

00:28:04.697 --> 00:28:05.720
(speaks in foreign language)

00:28:05.720 --> 00:28:08.400
It basically means,
everything is at its best

00:28:08.400 --> 00:28:09.790
when it's balanced.

00:28:09.790 --> 00:28:11.890
So I think balance is something.

00:28:11.890 --> 00:28:14.680
But who defines balance, parents, society,

00:28:14.680 --> 00:28:16.140
there's all those variables.

00:28:16.140 --> 00:28:18.890
I will say that people underestimate

00:28:18.890 --> 00:28:22.520
the nature of community
that is built digitally

00:28:22.520 --> 00:28:24.510
and the expanding of other views.

00:28:24.510 --> 00:28:26.220
The counterpoint to that is,

00:28:26.220 --> 00:28:28.360
especially in political times like this,

00:28:28.360 --> 00:28:30.260
people start going only into places

00:28:30.260 --> 00:28:32.170
where they hear affirmation
of their points of view.

00:28:32.170 --> 00:28:33.477
So there's a whole lot here.

00:28:33.477 --> 00:28:34.360
And this is, I think this

00:28:34.360 --> 00:28:36.973
is a four hour dinner
conversation in itself.

00:28:37.969 --> 00:28:40.302
(crosstalk)

00:28:41.140 --> 00:28:44.620
Let me (indistinct) that
I'm very passionate about.

00:28:44.620 --> 00:28:46.920
I'm enjoying something recently

00:28:46.920 --> 00:28:48.780
that I think about when I
think about Good Company,

00:28:48.780 --> 00:28:53.500
which is my inability to be canderous

00:28:53.500 --> 00:28:56.580
in my youth as a manager and leader,

00:28:56.580 --> 00:29:00.930
because I liked happy and
good and honey so much

00:29:00.930 --> 00:29:05.930
that I would kind of swallow
micro-little transgressions.

00:29:06.470 --> 00:29:08.450
Unfortunately though they
would ultimately lead

00:29:08.450 --> 00:29:10.840
to enough little drops where I would

00:29:10.840 --> 00:29:14.660
either at my worst, shock
and surprise someone

00:29:14.660 --> 00:29:17.420
in their firing and then
make myself feel better

00:29:17.420 --> 00:29:19.650
by saying how didn't
they realize they stunk,

00:29:19.650 --> 00:29:21.520
and didn't take on accountability.

00:29:21.520 --> 00:29:24.060
Over the last two to three years,

00:29:24.060 --> 00:29:25.940
I would say I was on a two out of 10

00:29:25.940 --> 00:29:28.340
when it came to candor as a leader

00:29:28.340 --> 00:29:29.420
because I overvalued...

00:29:29.420 --> 00:29:30.930
And it led to an, as you can imagine

00:29:30.930 --> 00:29:32.930
this is gonna make sense to
you, it led to entitlement.

00:29:32.930 --> 00:29:34.920
And a lot of things that
I had to work through.

00:29:34.920 --> 00:29:37.590
I think I'm in 6.5 land right now

00:29:37.590 --> 00:29:39.720
with an eye towards eight or nine.

00:29:39.720 --> 00:29:41.480
And I'm excited about that.

00:29:41.480 --> 00:29:44.180
In your journey to get you to a place

00:29:44.180 --> 00:29:46.040
of these incredible accomplishments

00:29:46.040 --> 00:29:48.810
and to be able to write
a book like Good Company.

00:29:48.810 --> 00:29:51.410
What part of the formula

00:29:51.410 --> 00:29:54.480
do you think you had to work on the most

00:29:54.480 --> 00:29:56.080
and get the reps in

00:29:56.080 --> 00:29:58.620
versus what came extremely natural to you

00:29:58.620 --> 00:30:01.573
that rounded out your ability
to execute Good Company?

00:30:03.490 --> 00:30:07.050
- Well, I would say it
relates to the first point

00:30:07.050 --> 00:30:08.100
that you were making.

00:30:09.813 --> 00:30:12.370
I'm a prostate cancer survivor

00:30:12.370 --> 00:30:15.640
and I just got another PSA and was zero.

00:30:15.640 --> 00:30:20.640
So I'm blessed, but I
would say, like cancer

00:30:20.710 --> 00:30:22.957
when you see it, you need
to deal with it seriously,

00:30:22.957 --> 00:30:25.190
you need to deal with it
early, if not, it's spreads.

00:30:25.190 --> 00:30:29.160
So I think when you're
dealing with human behavior

00:30:29.160 --> 00:30:32.990
within an organization,
when you see a bad behavior

00:30:32.990 --> 00:30:35.220
you need to address it, you
need to take it on seriously.

00:30:35.220 --> 00:30:37.960
It doesn't mean to fire
anybody, but it means that you,

00:30:37.960 --> 00:30:40.440
you address it seriously.
- (indistinct)

00:30:40.440 --> 00:30:43.270
- Yeah, and, because if
not, it is gonna spread.

00:30:43.270 --> 00:30:45.080
And it's not gonna spread
just with that person,

00:30:45.080 --> 00:30:46.620
but it will spread to other people.

00:30:46.620 --> 00:30:48.708
So the expression of bad Apple,

00:30:48.708 --> 00:30:51.840
(crosstalk)

00:30:51.840 --> 00:30:53.450
- That part of the honey empire was easy.

00:30:53.450 --> 00:30:57.040
The part was, I needed to learn a cadence

00:30:57.040 --> 00:31:00.210
to your point where I would
cut the cancer every time.

00:31:00.210 --> 00:31:02.473
It's why I've had what I've
had over these 25 years.

00:31:02.473 --> 00:31:03.400
- Yeah.

00:31:03.400 --> 00:31:06.090
- The process of getting to
the cutting was something

00:31:06.090 --> 00:31:07.830
that I was short on.

00:31:07.830 --> 00:31:09.540
I didn't do it in a way

00:31:09.540 --> 00:31:12.140
that I feel great about in hindsight.

00:31:12.140 --> 00:31:13.720
And I'm trying to build towards--

00:31:13.720 --> 00:31:16.810
- Well, I think that, that usually comes

00:31:16.810 --> 00:31:18.810
because it means somebody...

00:31:18.810 --> 00:31:21.190
The whole notion of sink or swimming,

00:31:21.190 --> 00:31:22.853
in business, I don't believe in.

00:31:22.853 --> 00:31:27.010
There's nothing worse than
watching somebody die drowning.

00:31:27.010 --> 00:31:29.630
So if you wanna put somebody
into sink or swim situation

00:31:29.630 --> 00:31:30.970
make sure they're properly trained

00:31:30.970 --> 00:31:33.500
make sure they can swim, make
sure they can see the shore

00:31:33.500 --> 00:31:35.520
make sure you can throw them a life guard,

00:31:35.520 --> 00:31:37.830
a life jacket rather, if you need to.

00:31:37.830 --> 00:31:42.580
So, you really wanna train people,

00:31:42.580 --> 00:31:45.610
put them in the best place
they can be for success

00:31:45.610 --> 00:31:47.664
and try to make sure
they have all the tools

00:31:47.664 --> 00:31:49.480
they need for that.

00:31:49.480 --> 00:31:52.310
If you reach a point, I
would say with anybody

00:31:52.310 --> 00:31:54.670
that despite all that,
they just don't get it,

00:31:54.670 --> 00:31:57.420
don't fit in, don't get your
values, don't wanna live it.

00:31:57.420 --> 00:32:00.960
So it's not, you're really
in many, in my experience

00:32:00.960 --> 00:32:02.640
is that you're doing them a favor.

00:32:02.640 --> 00:32:05.120
It's not fun for you to have
to beat them over the head

00:32:05.120 --> 00:32:07.020
for the next 10 years and telling them,

00:32:07.020 --> 00:32:08.480
constantly doing the wrong thing

00:32:08.480 --> 00:32:10.620
and they don't get it, they don't get it.

00:32:10.620 --> 00:32:12.440
Maybe they just don't
fit into your culture.

00:32:12.440 --> 00:32:15.290
So you do it in a kind
way, in a caring way.

00:32:15.290 --> 00:32:16.680
You let them go.

00:32:16.680 --> 00:32:19.590
You let them move on to find
a different organization

00:32:19.590 --> 00:32:22.300
that fits more with the
way they choose to operate.

00:32:22.300 --> 00:32:23.133
So--

00:32:23.133 --> 00:32:27.310
- When I asked you the
biggest growth of people that,

00:32:27.310 --> 00:32:31.890
because this is, we're getting
into very fun territory

00:32:31.890 --> 00:32:34.340
around emotional intelligence
and self awareness

00:32:34.340 --> 00:32:36.480
and things that really matter.

00:32:36.480 --> 00:32:37.313
- Right.

00:32:38.430 --> 00:32:39.720
- If I just say to you,

00:32:39.720 --> 00:32:42.100
think of the people that
you've seen grow the most

00:32:42.100 --> 00:32:43.940
through your years from pre-Depot,

00:32:43.940 --> 00:32:46.180
let alone Depot to today.

00:32:46.180 --> 00:32:47.640
Does it automatically go in your mind?

00:32:47.640 --> 00:32:49.170
Do you think of a Carol or a Suzy

00:32:49.170 --> 00:32:50.810
or a Rick or a John?
- Yeah.

00:32:50.810 --> 00:32:53.880
- Like, it's pretty, it's
pretty powerful to see that kind

00:32:53.880 --> 00:32:55.550
of emotional growth now.

00:32:55.550 --> 00:32:56.383
- Yeah, it is.

00:32:56.383 --> 00:32:58.140
And I will tell you that

00:33:00.530 --> 00:33:03.620
the hallmark to the success of Home Depot,

00:33:03.620 --> 00:33:06.450
it was built on products, assortment,

00:33:06.450 --> 00:33:09.280
service level product
knowledge, et cetera, et cetera.

00:33:09.280 --> 00:33:12.060
But the truth of the matter
is to get to 2200 stores

00:33:12.060 --> 00:33:13.660
where they are today.

00:33:13.660 --> 00:33:15.500
It's really built on just one thing.

00:33:15.500 --> 00:33:19.520
And that's understanding the
culture and living the culture.

00:33:19.520 --> 00:33:21.150
I'll tell you one brief story that

00:33:21.150 --> 00:33:25.370
when McKinsey was writing a
book on The War for Talent,

00:33:25.370 --> 00:33:28.982
their partner in Chicago, their
senior partner in Chicago,

00:33:28.982 --> 00:33:31.420
(indistinct) came to visit me last

00:33:31.420 --> 00:33:33.450
after we interviewed 200 of our associates

00:33:33.450 --> 00:33:36.300
in Canada, in United States and in Chile.

00:33:36.300 --> 00:33:38.540
We're operating stores there as well.

00:33:38.540 --> 00:33:40.750
And he said, I've talked
to 200 of your associates,

00:33:40.750 --> 00:33:44.270
a lot of engineers, cashiers,
salespeople, store managers,

00:33:44.270 --> 00:33:46.090
district managers, presidency divisions,

00:33:46.090 --> 00:33:47.650
merchants, et cetera.

00:33:47.650 --> 00:33:51.420
And every single one of
them talks about the culture

00:33:51.420 --> 00:33:53.710
exactly the same way, they
don't use the same words

00:33:53.710 --> 00:33:56.010
like it's memorized, and
you're just repeating it

00:33:56.010 --> 00:33:57.660
but they understand the essence of it.

00:33:57.660 --> 00:33:58.880
And they're living it.

00:33:58.880 --> 00:34:00.330
He said, I don't know how you do that.

00:34:00.330 --> 00:34:02.200
I've never met a company anywhere

00:34:02.200 --> 00:34:03.880
in a lot of our McKinsey experience

00:34:03.880 --> 00:34:05.790
that was able to able to do that.

00:34:05.790 --> 00:34:08.950
And I think that's the essence
of what makes us successful

00:34:08.950 --> 00:34:10.190
at Home Depot.

00:34:10.190 --> 00:34:12.800
And what has made all of our
businesses successful today

00:34:12.800 --> 00:34:16.200
is that the price to get
into the game of moving ahead

00:34:16.200 --> 00:34:18.210
and being a leader, being a manager

00:34:18.210 --> 00:34:23.040
is that you have to understand
and lead and be an ambassador

00:34:23.040 --> 00:34:26.030
for the culture of all of our businesses

00:34:26.030 --> 00:34:28.510
which we express there
in your own culture.

00:34:28.510 --> 00:34:31.250
So in your case, Gary, it means that

00:34:31.250 --> 00:34:33.350
sometimes the people
are the best technicians

00:34:33.350 --> 00:34:34.970
the best this, the best that.

00:34:34.970 --> 00:34:37.010
You can hire the best of those things,

00:34:37.010 --> 00:34:39.200
but you can't always hire somebody

00:34:39.200 --> 00:34:41.900
and train somebody to be the best

00:34:41.900 --> 00:34:44.410
of the culture that you want to reflect

00:34:44.410 --> 00:34:45.493
in your organization.

00:34:46.520 --> 00:34:51.520
- Arthur, how competitive?

00:34:52.160 --> 00:34:53.750
This is a fun question for me.

00:34:53.750 --> 00:34:56.880
How competitive were you in seeing

00:34:56.880 --> 00:34:58.653
how well the book was going to do?

00:34:59.780 --> 00:35:02.642
- Well, to be honest with you,

00:35:02.642 --> 00:35:05.010
all the proceeds are being donated

00:35:05.010 --> 00:35:07.900
to the National Civil
Rights Museum in Atlanta

00:35:07.900 --> 00:35:10.640
which I'm proud of,
it's a great place and--

00:35:10.640 --> 00:35:11.473
- Good news Arthur.

00:35:11.473 --> 00:35:14.870
I definitely didn't think
this was a financial exercise

00:35:14.870 --> 00:35:16.082
for you,
- No, it's not.

00:35:16.082 --> 00:35:20.930
- But winners win and I
know that you didn't need,

00:35:20.930 --> 00:35:22.213
I'm sure there were
some buddies you've had

00:35:22.213 --> 00:35:23.510
that have written business books

00:35:23.510 --> 00:35:25.587
that you wanted to sell one more than.

00:35:25.587 --> 00:35:26.420
- You know what?

00:35:26.420 --> 00:35:28.330
I only care about one thing, honestly.

00:35:28.330 --> 00:35:30.200
That the book gets out in the hands

00:35:30.200 --> 00:35:31.490
of as many people as it can.

00:35:31.490 --> 00:35:34.960
And primarily if I had to
choose, if you pressed me

00:35:34.960 --> 00:35:37.580
and you might, you press
me, well which population?

00:35:37.580 --> 00:35:39.450
I would say the younger population.

00:35:39.450 --> 00:35:41.370
The people, your listeners,

00:35:41.370 --> 00:35:43.410
the people that are
gonna be thinking about

00:35:43.410 --> 00:35:45.160
how do I live life with a purpose?

00:35:45.160 --> 00:35:46.970
How do I run a business with a purpose?

00:35:46.970 --> 00:35:48.730
How do I connect to not just myself

00:35:48.730 --> 00:35:50.930
and my family and my own circle of love,

00:35:50.930 --> 00:35:53.140
but to the greater need
of serving humanity

00:35:53.140 --> 00:35:54.400
in a broad sense?

00:35:54.400 --> 00:35:57.700
And, I feel strongly
that young people today

00:35:57.700 --> 00:36:00.730
are pressing on those
questions more and more.

00:36:00.730 --> 00:36:01.810
And I think we're blessed

00:36:01.810 --> 00:36:04.440
by having a younger
population that's doing that.

00:36:04.440 --> 00:36:07.840
So I would encourage,
that's who you listeners are

00:36:07.840 --> 00:36:11.610
and I would encourage them to
continue to press that way.

00:36:11.610 --> 00:36:14.500
And, they pick up the book or
trade it amongst themselves,

00:36:14.500 --> 00:36:15.333
amongst 10 people.

00:36:15.333 --> 00:36:17.920
So only is sold but 10 read
it, that's all I care about.

00:36:17.920 --> 00:36:18.890
Do they read it?

00:36:18.890 --> 00:36:20.190
Do they understand it?

00:36:20.190 --> 00:36:22.740
Do they understand they can
have that balance in life

00:36:22.740 --> 00:36:26.000
where they feel like, life
is being fulfilling to them.

00:36:26.000 --> 00:36:27.100
And they're getting confused

00:36:27.100 --> 00:36:29.500
between their work and their play.

00:36:29.500 --> 00:36:31.810
The whole Zen philosophies
that you don't go to work

00:36:31.810 --> 00:36:33.290
you gotta play every day.

00:36:33.290 --> 00:36:38.290
And, that's kind of what
we try to relate to.

00:36:38.800 --> 00:36:41.410
- I'm gonna have
(indistinct) who set this up,

00:36:41.410 --> 00:36:42.670
connect me with some of your book people.

00:36:42.670 --> 00:36:46.050
I think there's some
really fun gamification

00:36:46.050 --> 00:36:48.900
you can do in social that allows people

00:36:48.900 --> 00:36:51.820
to act on good behavior to win a copy

00:36:51.820 --> 00:36:55.280
of the book that I think would
probably make you very happy.

00:36:55.280 --> 00:36:56.840
'Cause you'd went on two fronts,

00:36:56.840 --> 00:36:58.610
there'd be good put into the world

00:36:58.610 --> 00:37:01.093
and another human would
consume it, and so--

00:37:01.093 --> 00:37:02.060
- That would be great
- I got the thought

00:37:02.060 --> 00:37:04.550
when you were just talking
that I wanna (indistinct)

00:37:04.550 --> 00:37:05.560
- Please, thank you.

00:37:05.560 --> 00:37:07.490
That'd be a blessing for me.

00:37:07.490 --> 00:37:10.740
- Arthur, I really,
really don't wanna go here

00:37:10.740 --> 00:37:13.520
because even though you
mentioned him as a friend.

00:37:13.520 --> 00:37:15.040
If you can imagine as a New York Jets fan

00:37:15.040 --> 00:37:18.300
that has watched every
single snap since 1982,

00:37:18.300 --> 00:37:20.380
I have an uncomfortably
difficult situation

00:37:20.380 --> 00:37:22.410
with the New England Patriots.

00:37:22.410 --> 00:37:23.970
This is something we also share

00:37:26.251 --> 00:37:29.210
what can we learn from a
moment like that Superbowl

00:37:29.210 --> 00:37:31.670
that must have hurt quite a bit.

00:37:31.670 --> 00:37:36.670
- It did, first of all, it's
certainly a good question.

00:37:38.420 --> 00:37:39.840
When you're leading 28 to three

00:37:39.840 --> 00:37:40.905
in the middle of third quarter,

00:37:40.905 --> 00:37:45.080
you almost think there's
almost no way to lose the game.

00:37:45.080 --> 00:37:46.201
I mean, I really wasn't thinking

00:37:46.201 --> 00:37:47.090
(crosstalk)

00:37:47.090 --> 00:37:47.923
- Yeah, that's right.

00:37:47.923 --> 00:37:49.820
To me, I'm such a cynical Jets fan,

00:37:49.820 --> 00:37:52.880
that if you are 49-3
with two minutes left.

00:37:52.880 --> 00:37:55.370
I am still not talking to anybody.

00:37:55.370 --> 00:37:56.480
- I am as well, all right?

00:37:56.480 --> 00:37:58.140
Even though it's was
20-3, I wasn't thinking--

00:37:58.140 --> 00:38:00.830
- There was a piece of
your brain thinking about

00:38:00.830 --> 00:38:03.870
some excitement, but you
were pretty locked into--

00:38:03.870 --> 00:38:05.780
- I was very locked in.

00:38:05.780 --> 00:38:08.260
And as the game started to
trend in the wrong direction

00:38:08.260 --> 00:38:10.830
as we got to the third and fourth quarter,

00:38:10.830 --> 00:38:13.490
it was interesting, my mind went to,

00:38:13.490 --> 00:38:15.740
I'm a father of six, a grandfather of six,

00:38:15.740 --> 00:38:20.200
is that, how do I be a
source of comfort, wisdom,

00:38:20.200 --> 00:38:24.253
counsel, support for my family,

00:38:25.257 --> 00:38:28.080
our players, our coaches, our fans.

00:38:28.080 --> 00:38:30.800
So it went away from myself

00:38:30.800 --> 00:38:35.110
to how do I help others get
through this horrific time?

00:38:35.110 --> 00:38:36.880
And that's what my focus was.

00:38:36.880 --> 00:38:39.300
I think I probably wasn't
until maybe a year later or so

00:38:39.300 --> 00:38:43.580
that I went through the
personal, paying myself

00:38:43.580 --> 00:38:46.240
because I know as you know,
you're a football fan.

00:38:46.240 --> 00:38:49.710
32 teams, it's very hard to
get back to the super bowl.

00:38:49.710 --> 00:38:52.050
It takes a single game
elimination in the playoffs,

00:38:52.050 --> 00:38:54.680
so, the ball is not round,
a lot of things can happen.

00:38:54.680 --> 00:38:55.961
And--

00:38:55.961 --> 00:38:59.580
- (indistinct), like when I'm
in my most down in business

00:38:59.580 --> 00:39:02.510
and things of that nature
because of my upbringing

00:39:02.510 --> 00:39:03.770
and the humble nature,

00:39:03.770 --> 00:39:06.360
and just the deep pain of
generational suppression

00:39:06.360 --> 00:39:10.120
in the Soviet union, I really
do care about something

00:39:10.120 --> 00:39:11.390
as silly as football.

00:39:11.390 --> 00:39:12.600
I mean, at least it's your team.

00:39:12.600 --> 00:39:14.420
This is like my, my fandom.

00:39:14.420 --> 00:39:16.280
And I get into a very serious place.

00:39:16.280 --> 00:39:17.113
I do find...

00:39:17.113 --> 00:39:20.170
By the way during COVID
I had a lot of friends...

00:39:20.170 --> 00:39:21.003
We are still in it.

00:39:21.003 --> 00:39:23.840
But like in the early kind
of like first 12, 16 weeks,

00:39:23.840 --> 00:39:24.790
I had a lot of friends going through

00:39:24.790 --> 00:39:26.120
some pretty difficult times.

00:39:26.120 --> 00:39:29.900
And I really tried to deploy
perspective to them of like

00:39:29.900 --> 00:39:32.920
just be grateful for what you
have, not what you've lost.

00:39:32.920 --> 00:39:36.370
I remember seeing a one friend
who was really struggling

00:39:36.370 --> 00:39:37.530
Hey, you do understand

00:39:37.530 --> 00:39:39.330
that if COVID started eight weeks earlier,

00:39:39.330 --> 00:39:42.360
Kobe Bryant would probably be
alive for the next 50 years.

00:39:42.360 --> 00:39:43.640
Let's put this into perspective.

00:39:43.640 --> 00:39:45.170
I understand you're worried about money.

00:39:45.170 --> 00:39:47.510
I understand you are
worried about a wedding,

00:39:47.510 --> 00:39:48.910
which is very important,

00:39:48.910 --> 00:39:51.310
but still let's really
put this in perspective.

00:39:51.310 --> 00:39:54.693
Do you tend to go into that place.

00:39:55.690 --> 00:39:57.810
- I tend not to go to
the dark place, Gary.

00:39:57.810 --> 00:40:00.040
What I tend to do is go to the place

00:40:00.040 --> 00:40:02.110
to be very much like you described.

00:40:02.110 --> 00:40:05.070
I mean, I'm blessed,
I have a great family.

00:40:05.070 --> 00:40:08.350
We have great businesses.

00:40:08.350 --> 00:40:10.300
Our golf business is an example

00:40:10.300 --> 00:40:12.570
which is just the counter, has taken off,

00:40:12.570 --> 00:40:14.860
I've never seen Comp sales in retail

00:40:14.860 --> 00:40:17.068
in my 50 years of retail
that we are experiencing.

00:40:17.068 --> 00:40:18.561
- (indistinct)

00:40:18.561 --> 00:40:19.740
It's just incredible.

00:40:19.740 --> 00:40:24.520
But to our own associates,
with football, soccer, ranches,

00:40:24.520 --> 00:40:25.353
which we closed.

00:40:25.353 --> 00:40:28.360
The first time in 100
years they've been closed.

00:40:28.360 --> 00:40:29.920
This is 2020.

00:40:29.920 --> 00:40:30.753
We'll get through this.

00:40:30.753 --> 00:40:31.750
It's a period of time.

00:40:31.750 --> 00:40:33.840
2021, we'll be fine.

00:40:33.840 --> 00:40:35.500
We'll go through a transition early 2021,

00:40:35.500 --> 00:40:37.760
and then we'll be fine going on.

00:40:37.760 --> 00:40:39.850
And keeping perspective of all the things

00:40:39.850 --> 00:40:41.557
we have blessings over.

00:40:41.557 --> 00:40:43.230
Everybody has blessings over.

00:40:43.230 --> 00:40:46.050
So I also know that like our foundation

00:40:46.050 --> 00:40:49.240
we've probably granted 40, $50 million

00:40:49.240 --> 00:40:53.480
just dealing with emergency
situations due to COVID

00:40:53.480 --> 00:40:55.700
in terms of food, safety, and health

00:40:55.700 --> 00:40:56.690
and things of that nature.

00:40:56.690 --> 00:40:59.560
We've helped, how many
non-profits just get

00:40:59.560 --> 00:41:01.210
through this period of time.

00:41:01.210 --> 00:41:03.020
Because these are great organizations

00:41:03.020 --> 00:41:06.410
that will do great work
in 2021 and beyond that.

00:41:06.410 --> 00:41:08.110
And so we need to help them

00:41:08.110 --> 00:41:09.290
get through this period of time.

00:41:09.290 --> 00:41:12.560
So, we had a meeting here last night,

00:41:12.560 --> 00:41:14.450
talking about a variety of
things with our leadership team.

00:41:14.450 --> 00:41:17.630
And I said, this is a year
you take care of your friends.

00:41:17.630 --> 00:41:18.743
You take care of your family.

00:41:18.743 --> 00:41:21.090
You take care of your
people that you care about,

00:41:21.090 --> 00:41:24.440
which for us is all of our
guests, all of our fans,

00:41:24.440 --> 00:41:27.260
all of our customers and
take care of your associates.

00:41:27.260 --> 00:41:29.560
And then you worry about
other things in 2021.

00:41:30.790 --> 00:41:33.650
It's just, that's the
attitude we have to have.

00:41:33.650 --> 00:41:35.450
- It's a tremendous way
to end this interview.

00:41:35.450 --> 00:41:36.880
Arthur, thank you for your time.

00:41:36.880 --> 00:41:38.450
- Thank you for your time, Gary.

00:41:38.450 --> 00:41:40.862
I appreciate it, God bless.

00:41:40.862 --> 00:41:41.960
(energetic music)

00:41:41.960 --> 00:41:42.793
- Hey, everybody on YouTube.

00:41:42.793 --> 00:41:45.030
First of all, thank you so
much, so humbled for your time.

00:41:45.030 --> 00:41:48.000
I don't wanna watch, but
time is the biggest asset.

00:41:48.000 --> 00:41:49.320
So thank you for watching that video.

00:41:49.320 --> 00:41:51.640
If you got some value out of that,

00:41:51.640 --> 00:41:53.890
there's plenty more where that came from.

00:41:53.890 --> 00:41:56.783
Feel free to check it out.

